{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd1ad1bd1aefa628","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n9\t  description: 'E006 (docs) lists the Investigation epic E001 in its dependencies\n10\t    with gated_by: null — an Investigation dependency without gated_by is a gate invisible\n11\t    to automated pipeline consumers, since the campaign outcome rewrites the documented\n12\t    model list and serving-docs scope. Correction: set gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n13\t    on E006 with a gate_failure_impact (action: rewrite — rescope the documented model\n14\t    list and serving coverage to the cleared set, or drop serving docs on a full no-go),\n15\t    mirroring E002; or, if a pure content-input edge is intended, remove E001 from\n16\t    E006 dependencies and carry the validated model list via E002 shipped support\n17\t    statement.'\n18\terror: null\n19\t---\n20\t## Review Summary\n21\t\n22\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n23\tRecommendation: revise\n24\t\n25\tContract validator: OK (skills/epic-decompose/scripts/validate_decomposition.py exited 0 — body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, and epic-size triggers all pass).\n26\t\n27\tTriage check: `triage` is null (full decomposition); 6 epics is expected and not penalized.\n28\t\n29\tThe decomposition is strong on coverage, boundaries, DAG construction, and signal calibration. One gating-metadata defect forces a zero on Type Correctness: the docs epic (E006) lists the Investigation epic (E001) in its `dependencies` without `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact`, so the gate is not machine-detectable by pipeline consumers.\n30\t\n31\t## Criterion Details\n32\t\n33\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n34\tAll four HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 → E001; HLR-2 → E002 + E003 (work-product-type split, not a priority split); HLR-3 → E004 + E005 (component/team boundary split); HLR-4 → E006. Traceability matrix is present and matches each epic's HLR Traceability section. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) gates E002 (P0); the only epics blocked by lower-priority work are E006, a docs-authoring epic exempt from priority inheritance (P1 correctly derived from strategy priority Major → P1). No priority collapse — no epic bundles deferrable lower-priority HLRs with P0 work.\n35\t\n36\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n37\tNo cycles; all edges reference existing epics. Frontmatter `dependencies` match the summary DAG exactly (E001→E002; E001/E002/E003/E004/E005→E006), and the diagram uses the dependency→dependent convention correctly. Parallelism rules are respected: E003 (konflux-onboarding) has no edge to E002 or any other general implementation epic in either direction — the coupling is correctly expressed as E002's \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion (Rule 25). E004/E005 run parallel with end-to-end HLR-3 verification as an AC on E004, matching the different-repos rule. E002's gate on E001 is properly recorded (`gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` with action and fallback). Critical path length 3 fits the M-standard heuristic (3-4). The E001→E006 edge is a valid content-input edge (validated model list); no missing data/artifact edges found in epic content — E002's consumption of pipeline outputs is content-reference coupling that correctly lives in an AC, not the DAG.\n38\t\n39\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n40\tEach component/team tuple is a separate epic: (Serving Runtimes, RHOAI Serving) ×3 with distinct work products (campaign, runtime productization, pipeline onboarding), (Training Operator, Distributed Workloads), (Distributed Workloads, Distributed Workloads), (Documentation, RHOAI Serving). No epic mixes code implementation with content authoring. No scope duplication between siblings — the E002/E003 and E004/E005 splits divide one HLR along work-product/component axes without rebuilding each other's surfaces. The validation campaign (E001) may exceed the ~2-week guideline, but it is one logical campaign with one report deliverable, and a concrete re-split trigger is recorded in the summary's Health Warnings (re-split along model-family lines on divergent tracks or model-set growth) — a managed, justified overage, not a boundary violation.\n41\t\n42\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n43\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (per-model go/no-go) determines the scope of E002 and the content of E006 — downstream epics exist that depend on it. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. E002's gating metadata is complete and correct. However, **E006 lists the Investigation epic E001 in its `dependencies` with `gated_by: null`**. Per the review contract, an Investigation dependency without `gated_by` is a major issue: by definition the Investigation outcome changes the downstream epic's scope (a no-go or smaller cleared set rewrites which models the documentation lists and whether serving docs exist at all), and automated pipeline consumers detect gates via the `gated_by` field on the direct `dependencies` list — this gate is invisible to them. E002 demonstrates the correct pattern for the identical dependency. Correction: set `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001` on E006 with a `gate_failure_impact` (e.g. action: rewrite — on a no-go or reduced cleared set, rescope the documented model list and serving coverage accordingly), or, if the decomposer intends a pure content-input edge rather than a gate, remove E001 from E006's `dependencies` and carry the model-list input via E002's shipped support statement — but the current state (Investigation in `dependencies`, no gate metadata) is the defect. Major issue → this criterion scores 0.\n44\t\n45\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n46\tEach epic carries the signal set matching its type: E001 has `investigation_signals` (5-signal 