or deliberately absent edge) | Rule | Rationale |\n47\t|-------|------|-----------|\n48\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's outcome (which models are cleared) determines the scope of the productization epic — a true gate with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` (a smaller cleared set rewrites the runtime's support scope; a no-go removes productizable scope). |\n49\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must describe what was built. |\n50\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must reflect the shipped build pipeline outputs. |\n51\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must cover the training support that landed. |\n52\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must cover the quota configuration that landed. |\n53\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 11 (content input) | The docs epic must state the validated model list — that list is the campaign's deliverable; depending only on the Implementation epics would leave this input unaccounted for. |\n54\t| no edge E003 ↔ E002 | Rule 10 (konflux-onboarding ∥ general implementation) | The pipeline onboarding and the runtime productization run in parallel; the coupling is expressed as the \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion on E002 (Rule 25), never as a DAG edge. |\n55\t| no edge E001 → E004/E005 | Rules 14/15 (independent repos) | Training enablement (training-operator, kueue repos) does not build against the serving validation campaign's output; HLR-3 is not stated as gated by the serving go/no-go. |\n56\t| no edge E004 ↔ E005 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | Training Operator and Kueue changes live in different repos; neither builds against the other's artifact. The end-to-end HLR-3 acceptance (multi-GPU job admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota) is verified as an acceptance criterion on the training epic, coordinated rather than serialized. |\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n59\t\n60\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n61\t|-------|----------|---------|-------|\n62\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing parity data and go/no-go scope. |\n63\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002, RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Split along work-product type: runtime productization (general implementation) vs. build-pipeline onboarding (konflux-onboarding recipe work) — different review cycles, mandated parallel by Rule 10. |\n64\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E004, RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Split along component/team boundary (Rule 1): Training Operator vs. Distributed Workloads (Kueue quota). |\n65\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | Docs-authoring epic. Its priority is P1, derived from the strategy frontmatter priority (Major → P1) per the docs-authoring priority exception — not inherited from HLR-4's P2 label. |\n66\t\n67\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No lower-priority HLR is bundled into a higher-priority epic (HLR-2 and HLR-3 splits are architectural/work-product boundaries, not priority splits).\n68\t\n69\t## Health Warnings\n70\t\n71\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy provides an Affected Components list without per-component change/owner detail, and has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Team assignments were inferred from the architecture-context owning teams (KServe and Serving Runtimes → RHOAI Serving; Training Operator and Distributed Workloads → Distributed Workloads). The docs epic is assigned to RHOAI Serving as the owner of the primary validated content — confirm with the documentation team.\n72\t- **Hardware access (open question 1)**: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes — procurement or partner-lab access — is unresolved. This is an externally-controlled availability precondition, not an acceptance criterion; it is carried as a risk on the validation campaign epic and affects the schedule of all hardware-dependent work, but it does not change which epics exist.\n73\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: whether AMD's runtime image releases align with our release trains is outside our control and has uncertain timing. The answer does not change which epics exist or what they do (the productization epic builds the ROCm variant either way; misalignment means pinning or rebuilding — fix-and-retry), so it is a schedule risk on the productization and training epics, not an Investigation epic and not a DAG edge.\n74\t- **Epic size (managed overage)**: the validation campaign (E001) is kept as one epic even though executing it across the full supported model set on shared hardware may run above the ~2-week epic guideline — it is one logical campaign with one report deliverable, and splitting it would duplicate the harness and hardware setup. Re-split trigger: re-split along model-family lines if execution findings show materially divergent validation tracks, or if the supported model set grows materially beyond the current estimate.\n75\t- **Docs epic priority**: E006 is P1 while its HLR-4 is P2 — this follows the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Major → P1), flagged here for reviewer visibility; it is not a priority inversion.\n76\t\n77\t## Tiered Delivery\n78\t\n79\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing is the upstream ROCm image release cadence (and, secondarily, hardware availability):\n80\t\n81\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: E001 — the validation campaign delivers risk-reduction value (functional/performance parity data and the go/no-go scope) regardless of upstream release-train alignment. E003 — build-pipeline preparation is recipe work independent of both the campaign outcome and upstream cadence.\n82\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-affected)**: E002 — productization ships for the models the campaign clears, and its shipping cadence is tied to upstream ROCm runtime image releases. E004/E005 — training enablement can proceed and land independently, but validated support statements for training follow the same upstream cadence risk.\n83\t\n84\t## Component Mapping Notes\n85\t\n86\t- All four affected components exist in `.context/architecture-context/` — no new-component onboarding chain is needed. The vLLM ROCm runtime is a new container image in an existing (already Konflux-onboarded) midstream repo, so per Rule 20 the pattern is image-build Implementation + konflux-onboarding in parallel, with no repo-onboarding.\n87\t- **KServe is passive**: no KServe code change is indicated — InferenceServices deploy whichever runtime is productized. Because the same team (RHOAI Serving) performs the validation, it is folded in as an acceptance criterion on E002 (\"cleared models deploy and serve via KServe InferenceServices on AMD GPU nodes\") rather than a separate epic.\n88\t- E001's component is `Serving Runtimes` (closest canonical name — the campaign validates the vLLM runtime); the campaign also exercises KServe deployment behavior, noted here as a span.\n89\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-823a9963175a9a23","agent_id":"ab11a49f255697c46","session_id":"985c5314-dfa6-4bcb-a27c-c01e92aa4111","uuid":"61a212be-67fb-4a75-a1d4-374af2f4fca5","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:15:06.563Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5ad5d9ecc2ded2e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
