{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a946cc4bd9514c89","type":"tool_result","content":"=== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001-ai-signals.md ===\n# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 (AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving)\n\nInvestigation epic — scored with the 5 investigation signals.\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| question_specificity | +1 | The question is concrete and enumerable: for each model in the supported model set, does vLLM ROCm serving match the CUDA baseline functionally (pass/fail) and in performance (throughput/latency numbers), with a defined go/no-go output. |\n| source_accessibility | 0 | No readable artifact contains or determines the answer — upstream repos and docs publish claimed ROCm support, but the platform's parity data for its supported model set does not exist anywhere yet; it must be produced by execution. |\n| local_runnability | 0 | The campaign requires physical AMD Instinct GPUs; vLLM ROCm serving cannot be exercised as a plain binary or pip/npm library on the machines available to an AI agent without that hardware. |\n| cluster_hardware_dependence | -2 | The gating question (functional/performance parity) itself requires physical AMD Instinct GPU nodes, which do not exist in the CI fleet today. |\n| human_judgment_required | -1 | The parity measurement itself is technical, but the go/no-go productization scope that the report feeds is a product/stakeholder decision requiring sign-off. |\n=== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E002-ai-signals.md ===\n# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 (Productize the vLLM ROCm serving runtime)\n\nImplementation epic — scored with the 9 implementation signals.\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| change_specificity | 0 | Scope is clear at the capability level (ROCm variant of the existing vLLM runtime, scoped to cleared models), but the strategy names no exact files, build targets, or manifests — the implementing team locates those in the midstream repo. |\n| pattern_precedent | +1 | The same midstream repo already productizes the CUDA vLLM runtime through the same release pipeline; adding an accelerator variant follows an in-codebase precedent, and upstream publishes ROCm builds of the same runtime. |\n| adapter_pattern | +1 | Upstream vLLM ROCm builds serve as the existing reference implementation this productization mirrors. |\n| existing_foundation | +1 | Extends the already-productized vLLM runtime and ServingRuntime machinery; no greenfield surface. |\n| open_questions | 0 | The implementation approach is fixed (build and ship the ROCm variant), but the cleared-model scope is deliberately unresolved until the validation campaign resolves — a gate on scope, not on design. |\n| external_dependency | -1 | Upstream ROCm runtime image cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities; release-train alignment is outside our control and may force pinning or rebuilding. |\n| human_process_gates | 0 | No human approval step beyond normal code/release review; the go/no-go decision is resolved upstream by the validation campaign gate. |\n| repo_access | +1 | The midstream vLLM repository (opendatahub-io/vllm) is an accessible platform repo. |\n| architecture_claims | +1 | The strategy's premise is corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/serving-runtimes.md`: \"only CUDA (NVIDIA) builds are currently productized — ROCm and Gaudi builds exist upstream but are not shipped or supported\", with KServe lifecycle via the ServingRuntime CRD matching the epic's deployment surface. |\n=== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E003-ai-signals.md ===\n# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 (Konflux build pipeline for the vLLM ROCm runtime image)\n\nImplementation epic — scored with the 9 implementation signals.\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| change_specificity | +1 | Konflux onboarding for a new image in an already-onboarded repo is a known recipe with a defined deliverable (pipeline config plus a green, attested image build). |\n| pattern_precedent | +1 | The midstream vLLM repository already runs Konflux pipelines for its CUDA images; the configuration to mirror exists in the same repo. |\n| adapter_pattern | +1 | Follows the existing Konflux onboarding recipe/reference configuration used by the repo's current image pipelines. |\n| existing_foundation | +1 | Builds on the repo's existing Konflux onboarding and release tasks; only the new ROCm image variant is added. |\n| open_questions | +1 | No unresolved design decisions affect the pipeline configuration; the image recipe follows the upstream ROCm build. |\n| external_dependency | 0 | The ROCm base images come from AMD's published registry as pipeline inputs — content references consumed at build time, not a coordination dependency. |\n| human_process_gates | 0 | No human approval step beyond normal pipeline/repo review. |\n| repo_access | +1 | The midstream vLLM repository is an accessible platform repo. |\n| architecture_claims | +1 | `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/serving-runtimes.md` records \"Konflux onboarded: yes\" for the component, corroborating that this epic extends existing onboarding rather than starting it. |\n=== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E004-ai-signals.md ===\n# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 (PyTorch ROCm training enablement for PyTorchJob workloads)\n\nImplementation epic — scored with the 9 implementation signals.\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| change_specificity | 0 | The capability is clearly stated (single- and multi-GPU PyTorchJobs on ROCm admitted through Kueue), but the strategy names no files, images, or operator changes — the team discovers the concrete surface in the training-operator repo. |\n| pattern_precedent | +1 | NVIDIA-GPU PyTorchJob train